---
title: Overview
summary: The server router for ogygia. SvelteKit's routing model (pages, layouts, loads, actions, endpoints and streamed pages) written as one typed table of values, with App['/path'] types and no codegen, plus a typed fetch client.
related:
  - router/guide
  - app/spa-router
  - postrender/overview
---

`@ogygia/router` is SvelteKit's routing model expressed as values. One ordinary module declares the route table, with pages, layouts, loads, actions and endpoints, and the types flow through it, so each component reads its `data`, `params` and `form` fully typed without a `$types` import. It is for routes that are not files: paths from a CMS or a database, generated pages, or a route table a package ships for an app to spread in.

It runs on the server. It matches the request, runs the load cascade and renders the page through its layouts. Moving between those pages in the browser is the job of `ogygia`'s [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router), which needs nothing from this package.

## Install

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/router
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { router } from '@ogygia/router/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [ogygia(), router(), sveltekit()]
});
```

The plugin tells the build that the modules importing `@ogygia/router` render pages, so the CSS of every component those pages reach is linked when they render.

## A first table

```ts title=src/lib/router.ts
import { routes, page } from '@ogygia/router';
import Home from '#lib/views/Home.svelte';
import Doc from '#lib/views/Doc.svelte';
import { docs } from '#lib/docs.server';

export const router = routes({
  '/': page(Home),
  '/docs/[slug]': page(Doc, { load: async (c) => ({ doc: await docs.get(c.params.slug) }) })
});

export type App = typeof router.$infer;
```

The table serves requests through its own handle, after ogygia's:

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { sequence } from '@sveltejs/kit/hooks';
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia/server';
import { router } from '#lib/router';

export const handle = sequence(ogygia.handle(), router.handle);
```

A component reads its typed data from `App`:

```svelte title=src/lib/views/Doc.svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import type { App } from '#lib/router';
  let { data }: App['/docs/[slug]'] = $props();
</script>

<h1>{data.doc?.data.title}</h1>
```

The table can also be mounted from a Kit catch-all route instead of the handle; [Mounting](/docs/router/guide#mounting) shows both.

## With other packages

The marks of `ogygia` work in the components a table renders, exactly as on a Kit page: an import with `wake` is an island, one with `render: 'deferred'` is a server island.

**[@ogygia/postrender](/docs/postrender/overview)** reads its `config` key from the table, at the same three levels as Kit (table, layout, page), so a route table decides which pages are stored:

```ts
import { routes, layout, page } from '@ogygia/router';

const marketing = layout('marketing', Shell, { config: { postrender: true } });

export const router = routes({
  ...marketing({ '/': page(Home), '/pricing': page(Pricing) }),
  '/account': page(Account, { config: { postrender: false } })
});
```

**[@ogygia/flags](/docs/flags/overview)** gates a whole route with `when()`, and picks a page component per visitor:

```ts
import { when } from '@ogygia/router';
import { checkoutV2, hero } from '#lib/flags';

routes({
  '/checkout-v2': when(checkoutV2, page(NewCheckout, { load })),
  '/': page(hero.pick({ control: Hero, bold: HeroBold }))
});
```

**[@ogygia/federation](/docs/federation/overview)** is built on this router: `mount(peer)` is a table entry that serves another team's app under your chrome.

